<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Magdeburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Magdeburg is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Otto I made it a centre of his rule and raised it to an archbishopric in 968, and its town law, Magdeburg law, was adopted by hundreds of towns across central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Its sack in 1631 was one of the worst massacres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/magdeburg.jpg" alt="Magdeburg" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olivier Cleynen · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Magdeburg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Magdeburg stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> river and is the capital of the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>. It is first mentioned in a document as Magadoburg in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulary">Capitulary</a> of Diedenhofen issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> in 805, and the name probably comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-high-german">Old High German</a> magado, big or mighty, and burg, fortress. The city marked its 1,200th anniversary in 2005.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> fortified the town in 919 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>. In 929 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> granted it to his English-born wife Edith as dower, and Otto himself was buried in the cathedral. The archbishops played a prominent part in the German colonisation of the Slavic lands east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>. In 1035 the city received a patent giving it the right to hold trade exhibitions, which formed the basis of the Magdeburg rights. In the thirteenth century Magdeburg joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. Fires in 1188 and 1207 destroyed much of it.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> preached there in 1524, after which the city left Roman Catholicism, and Matthias Flacius and his companions wrote the Magdeburg Centuries there. The city withstood a siege by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-wallenstein">Albrecht von Wallenstein</a> in 1629. In 1631 imperial troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-tserclaes-count-of-tilly">Johann Tserclaes, Count of Tilly</a>, stormed it, massacred the inhabitants and burned it, and only about 4,000 people remained after the war. Otto von Guericke, its mayor and a scientist, led the recovery. Under the Peace of Westphalia the city passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a> in 1680.</p><p>Magdeburg surrendered to French troops in 1806 and in 1815 became the capital of the Prussian Province of Saxony. A British air raid on 16 January 1945 destroyed much of the city centre. It belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> from 1949 to 1990, and in 1990 became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Magdeburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its dukes held counties, estates and advocacies over churches, led the Saxon army and presided over assemblies of Saxon nobles, but they shared power with great bishops, such as those of Bremen, Hildesheim and later <b>Magdeburg</b>, and with counts who held their offices directly from the king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Vilnius, granted <b>Magdeburg</b> rights in 1387, grew into a multi-faith city of Catholic, Orthodox and later Protestant churches and synagogues, and by the eighteenth century it was known as a centre of Jewish learning, the home of the scholar known as the Vilna Gaon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In its first centuries Brandenburg was a conquering frontier power, extending its rule over the Slavs east of the Elbe and contending with Pomerania, Poland, the Archbishopric of <b>Magdeburg</b> and Denmark.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Rome, Pope Leo X authorised a campaign of indulgence preaching in the lands of Albert of Brandenburg, archbishop of Mainz and <b>Magdeburg</b>, under an agreement that half the proceeds would repay the loan Albert had taken from the Fugger bank to pay the papacy for holding his sees.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Otto I founded a monastery at <b>Magdeburg</b> in 937 and, after years of negotiation with the Pope and the Archbishop of Mainz, raised it to an archbishopric in 968, with suffragans at Merseburg, Meissen, Zeitz (later Naumburg), Brandenburg and Havelberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/archbishopric-of-magdeburg">Archbishopric of Magdeburg</a></li><li><blockquote>The lords of Barby held lands on the Elbe near the mouth of the Saale, between <b>Magdeburg</b> and Anhalt, from the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-barby">County of Barby</a></li></ul></section>
